[list]
[*]Stands now dress for bed, alongside work and recreation. Tick Sleeping on a stand and whoever comes to that room to turn in changes on the way to bed, then changes back in the morning. A bedroom switches the row on by itself. A barracks does not and has to be ticked by hand, because a stand serves one colonist at a time: in a ten-bunk barracks the first sleeper claims it and the other nine go to bed in what they were already wearing. Sleeping, recreation and work types stay exclusive, so ticking one clears the other two.
[*]Deposit only is the setting for a rack beside the bed, and it is off by default. The stand issues nothing. A colonist heading for bed parks whatever its storage filter accepts, keeps the rest of their clothes on, and collects it in the morning. That is what gets a marine's power armour onto a mending stand overnight, with no pyjamas on the rack to swap into.
[*]Narrow the storage filter on a deposit-only stand. A new stand accepts nearly all apparel, so an untouched filter takes almost everything a colonist is wearing. One case is refused outright: if what the filter would take leaves a colonist without basic clothing, that colonist is skipped and the stand does nothing for them. A soldier who wears nothing under their armour needs clothes underneath, or pyjamas on the rack, before deposit only moves anything.
[*]Resting in bed is still a work type, and it is not what the Sleeping row does. That one is vanilla's bed rest for recuperating from an injury or illness, and a doctor's orders are what put a colonist in bed under it. The Sleeping row fires on ordinary bedtime. A stand set to Sleeping ignores medical bed rest; a stand carrying the work type ignores ordinary bedtime.
[*]A stand whose trigger comes from its room now shows the work-type list, with a note naming the room as the source. Ticking a work type overrides it. That list used to be hidden on those stands, so putting a bedroom or rec-room stand onto a work type meant switching it off first and starting again.
[*]Fixed: colonists could be pulled out of bed to change for recreation. Vanilla gives bedridden colonists joy they can take lying down so they stay put, and the guard against interrupting that asked whether the colonist was in bed. That question reads the colonist's current job, and the game clears the current job before handing over the new one, so the answer came back no every time. The guard never fired. It now looks for a bed on the cell the colonist is standing on.
[/list]
